Sure, here is a simple example of a hypnotic animation in Shadertoy:

```glsl
void mainImage( out vec4 fragColor, in vec2 fragCoord )
{
    vec2 uv = fragCoord.xy / iResolution.xy;

    float time = iTime * 0.5;

    vec2 p = vec2(sin(uv.x * 10.0 + time), cos(uv.y * 10.0 + time));

    float c = smoothstep(-0.2, 0.2, sin(p.x * 10.0) * cos(p.y * 10.0));

    fragColor = vec4(vec3(c), 1.0);
}
```

This code creates a simple hypnotic animation by using sine and cosine functions to manipulate the UV coordinates of each pixel. The smoothstep function is used to create a smooth transition between colors. You can further customize this animation by adjusting the frequency and amplitude of the sine and cosine functions, as well as experimenting with different color gradients and effects.

